WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.064
Flask commonly uses Jinja templates to
combine server-side data with HTML.

2
00:00:08.144 --> 00:00:13.935
Jinja can provide autoescaping in
appropriate contexts,

3
00:00:14.015 --> 00:00:20.752
but developers still need to understand
where data is being placed.

4
00:00:20.832 --> 00:00:25.678
HTML text, attributes, URLs, JavaScript,
JSON,

5
00:00:25.758 --> 00:00:30.604
and style contexts have different safety
rules.

6
00:00:30.684 --> 00:00:38.248
A value that is harmless as plain text may
become risky if it is inserted into a

7
00:00:38.328 --> 00:00:42.110
script block, an event handler, a URL,

8
00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:47.627
or a client-side template without the
right handling.

9
00:00:47.707 --> 00:00:56.098
Cross-site scripting is a response safety
problem where untrusted content becomes

10
00:00:56.178 --> 00:01:02.442
executable or behavior-changing content in
a user's browser.

11
00:01:02.522 --> 00:01:08.550
The defensive principle is context-aware
output handling:

12
00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:16.076
validate what data is allowed, keep
user-generated content constrained,

13
00:01:16.156 --> 00:01:19.347
rely on safe template behavior,

14
00:01:19.427 --> 00:01:24.982
and avoid patterns that mark untrusted
content as safe.

15
00:01:25.062 --> 00:01:30.026
Security headers can add useful defense in
depth,

16
00:01:30.106 --> 00:01:37.315
but they do not replace correct rendering
decisions in the application.

17
00:01:37.395 --> 00:01:45.432
CSRF is concerned with unwanted cross-site
requests causing a user's browser to

18
00:01:45.512 --> 00:01:50.240
perform an action in an authenticated
context.

19
00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:58.947
Flask applications that use cookies for
authentication should think carefully about

20
00:01:59.027 --> 00:02:02.691
state-changing forms and requests.

21
00:02:02.771 --> 00:02:08.681
CSRF protection at a high level uses
unpredictable tokens,

22
00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:13.607
same-site cookie behavior, method
discipline,

23
00:02:13.687 --> 00:02:20.660
and clear separation between safe reads
and state-changing actions.

24
00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:24.049
APIs may use different patterns,

25
00:02:24.129 --> 00:02:32.520
but the underlying question is whether the
request was intentionally authorized by

26
00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:35.673
the user and application flow.

27
00:02:35.753 --> 00:02:43.790
Response safety also includes redirects,
data minimization, and error output.

28
00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:51.907
Redirect targets should be constrained so
the application does not send users to

29
00:02:51.987 --> 00:02:57.069
untrusted destinations based on unchecked
input.

30
00:02:57.149 --> 00:03:05.777
Responses should not include fields simply
because they are convenient to serialize.

31
00:03:05.857 --> 00:03:09.875
Templates, JSON responses, downloads,

32
00:03:09.955 --> 00:03:15.629
and error pages should reveal only what
the caller needs.

33
00:03:15.709 --> 00:03:24.100
Safe response design reduces both
browser-side risk and accidental data exposure.
